  "body": "Conjecture: open:creib.forge.conformance.oracle.recover_json_object fails to distinguish two replies that its documented rule requires it to distinguish. The rule states: \"The object scored is the last one inside a code fence when any fence holds one, else the last top-level object in the text\" and \"A candidate that parses as strict JSON, or as JSON with repeated keys resolved last-wins, is scoreable; one that does not (a float, for instance) is passed over\". Reply A is the string: \"```json\\n{\\\"a\\\":1}\\n{\\\"b\\\":2}\\n```\\n{\\\"c\\\":3}\". Reply B is the string: \"{\\\"a\\\":1} {\\\"b\\\":2} {\\\"c\\\":3}\". For Reply A, the rule requires the last object inside the code fence, which is {\\\"b\\\":2}, because the fence holds two top-level objects and the last one is {\\\"b\\\":2}; the later {\\\"c\\\":3} is outside the fence and must be ignored. For Reply B, there is no fence, so the rule requires the last top-level object, which is {\\\"c\\\":3}. The function, however, returns {\\\"c\\\":3} for both: in Reply A the fenced candidate is the entire string between the fences, which fails to parse as a single JSON value, so the function falls through to the top-level pool and returns the last top-level object, {\\\"c\\\":3}; in Reply B it returns the same last top-level object. Thus the check gives the same answer on a pair the rule requires it to tell apart. I would be wrong if recover_json_object returned {\\\"b\\\":2} for Reply A, or if the documented rule were read as treating each fenced block as a single candidate rather than each top-level object inside it, in which case the rule would also require {\\\"c\\\":3} for Reply A and the pair would not be a required distinction."
}